  {
   "index": 15,
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "mntl-sc-block_11-0",
   "class": "comp mntl-sc-block finance-sc-block-html mntl-sc-block-html",
   "landmarks": [
    "main",
    "article"
   ],
   "textLength": 233,
   "text": "A pre-qualification letter usually doesn’t carry the same weight to home sellers as a verified pre-approval letter. Still, sometimes buyers prefer to start with getting pre-qualified, as this can be useful for narrowing down lenders.",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 0,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 1,
    "repeats": 1,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
   "geometry": {
    "top": 2881,
    "height": 81,
    "widthFraction": 0.41,
    "leftFraction": 0.26,
    "pageFraction": 0.3,
    "aboveFold": false
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 16,
   "tag": "p",
   "id": "mntl-sc-block_13-0",
   "class": "comp mntl-sc-block finance-sc-block-html mntl-sc-block-html",
   "landmarks": [
    "main",
    "article"
   ],
   "textLength": 377,
   "text": "“If a buyer may not be ready to move forward immediately, but wants a preliminary review to get an idea of where they stack up financially while providing less information, they might only want or need pre-qualification,” Crescenzo added. “If a client didn’t want a credit inquiry, but wanted a soft pull, this could also be a reason to ask for a process that’s less involved.”",
